        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b47afae8-ed66-570d-8c4e-aacf8d695d3e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: William T. Anderson, Jesse James, Bob Ford, Mary \"Mother\" Jones, William Quantrill\n- Events: Centralia Massacre, American Civil War\n- Places: Centralia, Missouri; Huerfano County Courthouse and Jail\n- Roles: pro-Confederate guerrilla leaders, Union soldiers, labor leader\n\nFacts:\n- On September 27, 1864, the Centralia Massacre occurred at Centralia, Missouri during the American Civil War.\n- The Centralia Massacre involved the capture and execution of twenty-four unarmed Union soldiers by pro-Confederate guerrilla leader William T. Anderson.\n- In 1864, followers of William Quantrill and William T. Anderson, including Jesse James, were accused of participating in atrocities against Union soldiers and civilian abolitionists, including the Centralia Massacre.\n- William T. Anderson, known as \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson, lived from 1840 to October 26, 1864 and was one of the deadliest and most brutal pro-Confederate guerrilla leaders in the American Civil War.\n- Huerfano County Courthouse and Jail held Bob Ford, who killed Jesse James, and labor leader Mary \"Mother\" Jones.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "348e754e-4a2a-5dad-b0d4-d01fbe3aad29",
        "text": "Page: Centralia Massacre (Missouri). Sentence: The Centralia Massacre was an incident during the American Civil War in which twenty-four unarmed Union soldiers were captured and executed at Centralia, Missouri on September 27, 1864 by the pro-Confederate guerrilla leader William T. Anderson.\nPage: William T. Anderson. Sentence: William T. Anderson (1840 – October 26, 1864)—known as \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson—was one of the deadliest and most brutal pro-Confederate guerrilla leaders in the American Civil War.\nPage: Jesse James. Sentence: As followers of William Quantrill and \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson, they were accused of participating in atrocities against Union soldiers and civilian abolitionists, including the Centralia Massacre in 1864.\nPage: Huerfano County Courthouse and Jail. Sentence: The jail held Bob Ford, who killed Jesse James, and labor leader Mary \"Mother\" Jones.",
